// A missing import must NOT be reported as a compiler bug.
//
// `0.9.287` added a backstop: a MARKED `give`/`copy` whose SOURCE class did not resolve, into a
// destination that OWNS something, is refused as a compiler bug — because after `0.9.283`/`0.9.284` every
// named-lvalue shape has a resolver, so reaching that branch means the compiler failed, not the author.
//
// ⚠️ It MISFIRED the first time it met a real mistake. One absent `import { std::collections::DynamicArray }`
// leaves `bytes` with no class while the callee's parameter type resolves fine — known destination,
// unresolved source, which is exactly the backstop's shape — so it blamed the compiler and buried the real
// message under a "please report it" the reader cannot act on. It now stays quiet once anything has been
// diagnosed.
//
// ⚠️ WHAT PINS THIS IS THE POSITION LIST IN `DIAGNOSTIC_LINES`, NOT THE MESSAGE. Both diagnostics below are
// the author's and both are correct; the regression would be a THIRD one appearing on the `give` line, and
// an extra position is what fails this fixture. A `.msg` on the first error alone would not have noticed.
import { std::serialization::binary::kbin::deserializeKbinPositionalBuffer, std::memory::Shared };

@generate(Serializable, Deserializable)
type resource Node {
    @field int32 v;
    @field Optional<Shared<Node>> next;
    public ctor make(int32 v) { this.v = v; this.next = Optional::None; }
}

fn int32 main() {
    DynamicArray<uint8> bytes = DynamicArray.empty();     // `DynamicArray` deliberately NOT imported
    Result<Shared<Node>, Owned<Error>> r = deserializeKbinPositionalBuffer::<Shared<Node>>(src: give bytes);
    return 0;
}
